{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalParam","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.GenericParameterReal"],"declarations":40,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRepFin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRepFin","kind":"def","line":1796,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1796-L1803","doc":"**The seminormal representation on `Fin (dLambda lam)`.**  `seminormalRep` transported\nalong `Fintype.equivFin`, giving the matrix algebra in the indexing\n`hoefsmit_seminormal_rep` states it in. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_ne_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_ne_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":1749,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1749-L1786","doc":"**A generator that moves any tableau is not a scalar matrix.**\n\nNeeds no hypothesis on `q` whatever.  The reason is that `offDiag` takes the plain value `1`\non the non-negative branch, so of the two off-diagonal entries `(s_k·T, T)` and `(T, s_k·T)`\n— which sit at distances `d` and `-d` — **at least one is exactly `1`**, whichever sign `d`\nhas.  A scalar matrix has both equal to `0`.\n\nNote this does *not* go through `offDiag ≠ 0` at the moved entry: that can genuinely vanish,\nsince `offDiag q (-1) = 0`.  It is the *pair* that cannot both vanish. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_σ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep_σ","kind":"theorem","line":1743,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1743-L1747","doc":"`seminormalRep` sends the generator `σ_i` to the matrix `ρ(σ_{i+1})`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalRep","kind":"def","line":1721,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1721-L1741","doc":"**The general-`λ` Hoefsmit seminormal representation.**\n\n`H_{n+1}(q) →ₐ[ℝ] M_{SYT μ}(ℝ)` for any shape `μ` with `|μ| = n + 1`, built from the three\nrelations proved above through the universal property `Hecke.lift`.  Its dimension is\n`#SYT μ`, the Specht dimension of `μ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalGen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.seminormalGen","kind":"def","line":1716,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1716-L1719","doc":"The generator matrices re-indexed for `Hecke.lift`: `σ_i ↦ ρ(σ_{i+1})`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid","kind":"theorem","line":1657,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1657-L1702","doc":"**The braid relation, unconditionally.**\n\n`ρ(σ_k)ρ(σ_{k+1})ρ(σ_k) = ρ(σ_{k+1})ρ(σ_k)ρ(σ_{k+1})` on all of `SYT μ`, arbitrary shape, no\nhypothesis on the orbit.\n\nThe proof dispatches on three flags — does `σ_k` move `T`, does `σ_{k+1}`, does `σ_{k+1}`\nmove `s_k·T` — to the seven shape theorems.  Every remaining hypothesis those theorems ask\nfor is *derived* rather than assumed, via the classification iffs: `hBk` from `hAl`\n(`swapAdmissible_cross_iff` — they are the same condition on the cells of `k` and `k+2`), and\nthe two length-`2` admissibilities from `T`'s own (`swapAdmissible_word_left_iff` /\n`_right_iff`).\n\nWith `genMat_quadratic` and `genMat_far_comm`, this completes the three Hecke relations\nunconditionally. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_fixed_succ_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_fixed_succ_three","kind":"theorem","line":1540,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1540-L1655","doc":"**Size-`3` orbit, `T` at the `σ_k`-fixed end.**  `σ_{k+1}` moves `T`, `σ_k` fixes it, but\n`s_{k+1}·T` *is* `k`-admissible, so the orbit runs `T → s_{k+1}·T → s_k s_{k+1}·T` and stops.\n\nThe mirror of `genMat_braid_of_succ_fixed_three`, and — consistent with the rule that\ndeletion tracks which columns are scalar — it deletes on the **left** where that one deleted\non the right, with the degeneracy at `p = ±1` rather than `r = ±1`.  The third row again\ncloses by `ring`, this time via `axialDist_swapTableau_word_right`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_middle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_middle","kind":"theorem","line":1417,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1417-L1538","doc":"**Size-`3` orbit, `T` in the middle.**  Both generators move `T`, but neither `s_k·T` nor\n`s_{k+1}·T` extends further, so the orbit is `{s_{k+1}·T, T, s_k·T}` and stops.\n\nThis is the position that shares its leading flags with a generic size-`6` orbit, and it is\nalso the one that needs **no deletion at all**: its three rows are `braid_diag_identity`,\n`braid_rowA_identity` and `braid_rowB_identity` *unchanged*.\n\nThat is worth stating because it inverts the expectation set by the smaller orbits.  Sizes\n`1` and `2` were the generic identity with terms removed, licensed by scalar degeneracy;\nhere nothing is removed and no degeneracy is used — neither generator fixes `T`, so no\ndistance is `±1`.  The orbit is smaller than generic purely because the two length-`2` words\nfail to exist, and the rows that *do* exist are the generic ones. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_succ_fixed_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_succ_fixed_three","kind":"theorem","line":1306,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1306-L1415","doc":"**Size-`3` orbit, `T` at the `σ_{k+1}`-fixed end.**  `σ_k` moves `T`, `σ_{k+1}` fixes it,\nbut `s_k·T` *is* `(k+1)`-admissible, so the orbit runs `T → s_k·T → s_{k+1}s_k·T` and stops.\n\nCompared with the size-`2` case `genMat_braid_of_succ_fixed`, the `T` and `s_k·T` rows carry\nthe *same* two identities; what is new is the third row, which closes by `ring` alone once\n`axialDist_swapTableau_word_left` supplies `d_k(s_{k+1}s_k·T) = r`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_fixed_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_fixed_succ","kind":"theorem","line":1202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1202-L1287","doc":"**The braid on the mirror size-`2` orbit** — `σ_{k+1}` moves `T`, `σ_k` fixes both `T`\nand `s_{k+1}·T`, so the orbit is `{T, s_{k+1}·T}`.\n\nThe mirror of `genMat_braid_of_succ_fixed`, and it deletes the *other* term: here the **left**\nside lacks `a₃(1 + a₁a(-p))`, and the degeneracy that licenses it is at `p = ±1` rather than\n`r = ±1`.  The moved row is `braid_rowB_identity` verbatim. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_succ_fixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_succ_fixed","kind":"theorem","line":1104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1104-L1200","doc":"**The braid relation on a size-`2` orbit** — `σ_k` moves `T`, `σ_{k+1}` fixes both `T`\nand `s_k·T`, so the orbit is exactly `{T, s_k·T}`.\n\nOnly two rows are non-zero, and they close differently:\n\n* the `s_k·T` row is `braid_rowA_identity` verbatim — the same identity as in the generic\n  case;\n* the `T` row is the generic diagonal identity **minus** the term `a₃·(1 + a₂a(-r))`, which\n  the right-hand side does not produce here.  The two sides match only because that factor\n  is `0` — `one_add_seminormalParam_mul_neg_of_scalar`, available because `σ_{k+1}` fixing\n  `T` forces `r = ±1` by the trichotomy.\n\nSo the smaller orbit is not a degenerate special case of the generic one: it is the generic\nidentity with a term deleted, and the deletion is legitimate exactly because of the scalar\ndegeneracy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_both_fixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_of_both_fixed","kind":"theorem","line":1070,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L1070-L1102","doc":"**The braid relation on a size-`1` orbit** — both generators fix `T`.\n\nBoth columns are scalar, so each triple product collapses to a single entry times\n`a(d_k)a(d_l)`, and the two sides differ by `a(d_k)a(d_l)·(a(d_k) - a(d_l))`.\n\nThat is *not* zero for arbitrary scalars: at `d = ±1` the parameter is `q` or `-q⁻¹`, and\nthe mixed product would give `-(q + q⁻¹) ≠ 0`.  The identity holds because the mixed\nconfiguration cannot occur — `axialDist_eq_of_not_admissible` — which is the only place in\nthe whole construction where the braid depends on a *geometric* fact about `μ` rather than\non the parameter algebra. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_generic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_generic","kind":"theorem","line":847,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L847-L1068","doc":"**The braid relation on a generic orbit, at the tableau level.**\n\n`ρ(σ_k)ρ(σ_{k+1})ρ(σ_k) = ρ(σ_{k+1})ρ(σ_k)ρ(σ_{k+1})` in column `T`, for a `T` whose\n`⟨s_k, s_{k+1}⟩`-orbit is the full size-`6` one — that is, all six admissibilities hold.\n\nThe six rows split three and three: `D`, `E`, `F` are identities of the ambient ring (the\ntwo sides are the same product reordered), while `T`, `A`, `B` each reduce to the single\nrelation `seminormalParam_rel`, with multiples `(a₂ - a₁)`, `-off(p)`, `+off(r)`.\n\nNon-generic orbits — where one of the five non-`T` words fails to be `SwapAdmissible` and\nterms drop — are **not** covered here; that case tree is the remaining work on\n`hoefsmit_seminormal_rep`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_apply_diag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_apply_diag","kind":"theorem","line":745,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L745-L845","doc":"**The braid's diagonal entry, at the tableau level.**\n\nThe first row of the braid instantiated against the two expansions — `braid_diag_identity`\nwired to actual `genMat` entries rather than to abstract parameters.\n\nThe hypotheses are the six admissibilities of a generic orbit.  They are *not* redundant\neven for this single row: the `(T, D)` and `(T, E)` entries are only known to vanish once\n`D` and `E` exist as tableaux, which is what `hDk` and `hEl` provide. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.braid_rowB_identity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.braid_rowB_identity","kind":"theorem","line":711,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L711-L715","doc":"**The `s_{k+1}·T` row** — the mirror of `braid_rowA_identity`, differing only in the\nsign of the multiple of the relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.braid_rowA_identity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.braid_rowA_identity","kind":"theorem","line":705,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L705-L709","doc":"**The `s_k·T` row.**  Left gives `off(p)·(a₁a₂ + a₃·a(-p))`, right gives `off(p)·a₃a₂`;\nthe difference is `off(p)` times the relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.braid_diag_identity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.braid_diag_identity","kind":"theorem","line":683,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L683-L703","doc":"**The braid's diagonal row, as pure algebra — and the relation is exactly what closes\nit.**\n\nResolving the two expansions above at row `T'  = T` leaves, with `a₁ = a(p)`, `a₂ = a(r)`,\n`a₃ = a(p+r)` and `c = q - q⁻¹`:\n\n* from the left, `a₁²a₂ + a₃·off(-p)·off(p)`;\n* from the right, `a₁a₂² + a₃·off(-r)·off(r)`;\n\nand `offDiag_mul_neg` turns each `off·off` into `1 + a(d)·a(-d) = 1 + a(d)(c - a(d))`\n(`seminormalParam_add_neg`).  The difference of the two sides factors as\n\n  `(a₁ - a₂)·[a₁a₂ - a₃(a₁ + a₂ - c)]`\n\nwhose second factor is `seminormalParam_rel` — vanishing identically.  Note the first factor\nis *not* needed: the identity holds whether or not `a₁ = a₂`, so no genericity assumption\nabout the orbit enters here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_rhs_expand","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_rhs_expand","kind":"theorem","line":651,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L651-L681","doc":"**The braid's right-hand side, expanded onto the same orbit.**  The mirror of\n`genMat_braid_lhs_expand`, with `k` and `k+1` exchanged; the third parameter is again\n`a(p + r)`, now by `axialDist_swapTableau_succ`.\n\nComparing the two expansions is what the braid reduces to — and comparing them needs the\norbit-size classification, because the row cases turn on which of the four named tableaux\ncoincide. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_lhs_expand","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_braid_lhs_expand","kind":"theorem","line":618,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L618-L649","doc":"**The braid's left-hand side, expanded onto the orbit.**  Two applications of\n`genMat_mul_apply` reduce `ρ(σ_k)ρ(σ_{k+1})ρ(σ_k)` at column `T` to four entries of\n`ρ(σ_k)` — at `T`, at `s_{k+1}T`, at `s_kT`, and at `s_{k+1}s_kT` — with coefficients built\nfrom `a(p)`, `a(r)`, `a(p+r)` and the matching `offDiag`s.\n\nThe `a(p + r)` is where the third parameter enters, supplied by\n`axialDist_succ_swapTableau`: `σ_{k+1}` acting on `s_kT` reads the *sum* of the two\ndistances, not a new quantity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_far_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_far_comm","kind":"theorem","line":424,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L424-L581","doc":"**Far commutation, on all of `SYT μ` at once.**\n\n`ρ(σ_k) ρ(σ_l) = ρ(σ_l) ρ(σ_k)` for `|k - l| ≥ 2`, arbitrary shape `μ`.\n\nBoth products are supported on the same four tableaux `{T, s_k·T, s_l·T, s_k s_l·T}`, and\neach pairs them the same way — because a far swap at `l` changes nothing `σ_k` reads\n(`axialDist_swapTableau_of_far`, `swapAdmissible_far_iff`) and the two actions commute\n(`swapTableau_comm`).  The four rows then match term by term, with the factors merely\ntransposed.\n\n**No hypothesis on `q`.**  The quadratic needed `a(d) + a(-d) = q - q⁻¹` and hence\n`q > 0`, `q ≠ 1`; far commutation never combines two parameters, so it holds for every\nreal `q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.swapTableau_far_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.swapTableau_far_ne","kind":"theorem","line":400,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L400-L422","doc":"**Two far swaps of the same tableau land in different places.**  At the cell holding\n`k`, `s_k·T` carries `k+1` while `s_l·T` still carries `k`.\n\nThis is the distinctness the four-element support `{T, s_k·T, s_l·T, s_k s_l·T}` rests on:\nfrom it, `T ≠ s_k s_l·T` follows by applying `s_k` to both sides. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_mul_apply_of_not_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_mul_apply_of_not_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":391,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L391-L398","doc":"The scalar-column case of `genMat_mul_apply`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_mul_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_mul_apply","kind":"theorem","line":377,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L377-L389","doc":"**One factor peeled off a product.**  Multiplying on the right by `genMat q l` replaces\ncolumn `T` by the two-term combination its support dictates.  Iterating this is how a\nproduct of generators is evaluated: three applications turn the braid's triple product into\na sum over the `⟨s_k, s_{k+1}⟩`-orbit of `T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.sum_mul_genMat_column_of_not_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.sum_mul_genMat_column_of_not_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":365,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L365-L375","doc":"The column of a tableau that `σ_k` fixes is the single diagonal entry. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":259,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L259-L344","doc":"**The Hecke quadratic relation, on all of `SYT μ` at once.**\n\n`ρ(σ_k)² = (q - q⁻¹)·ρ(σ_k) + 1` — the relation `Hecke.σ_quadratic` imposes, in the\nnormalisation `σ² = (q - q⁻¹)σ + (q·q⁻¹)·1`.\n\nThe proof is a two-case split on `SwapAdmissible`, which is the trichotomy of\n`TableauCells`:\n\n* **`σ_k` fixes `T`** (axial distance `±1`).  The column is the single entry `a(±1)`, which\n  `seminormalParam_one` / `_neg_one` evaluate to `q` resp. `-q⁻¹` — the two roots of\n  `X² - (q - q⁻¹)X - 1`.\n* **`σ_k` moves `T`.**  The column is `{T, s_k·T}`, and the `2 × 2` block it spans has\n  trace `a(d) + a(-d) = q - q⁻¹` (`seminormalParam_add_neg`, using\n  `axialDist_swapTableau`) and determinant `-1` (`offDiag_mul_neg`).  Cayley–Hamilton\n  does the rest.\n\nNo block-diagonal decomposition is constructed or needed: the block structure is read off\nthe entry rules column by column. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.sum_mul_genMat_column","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.sum_mul_genMat_column","kind":"theorem","line":239,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L239-L257","doc":"**The column support of `genMat`.**  Column `T` has its nonzero entries at `T` itself\nand — when `σ_k` moves `T` — at `s_k·T`; nowhere else.  Summing any weight against that\ncolumn therefore collapses to two terms. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.swapTableau_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.swapTableau_ne","kind":"theorem","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L224-L237","doc":"**The swap moves every tableau it is defined on.**  `s_k·T` differs from `T` already at\nthe cell holding `k`, which carries `k+1` afterwards. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.zpow_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.zpow_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L202-L222","doc":"**`q ≠ 0` and `q² ≠ 1` is exactly the condition for a real `q` not to be a root of\nunity**, hence for `q ^ (-2d) ≠ 1` at every nonzero axial distance — which is what keeps\n`seminormalParam` off its pole.\n\nThis is *sharp over `ℝ`*, and that matters for the hypotheses of the whole file.  `q^m = 1`\nwith `m ≠ 0` forces `|q| = 1`, hence `q = ±1`, hence `q² = 1`; so no further condition is\navailable to assume and none is needed.  In particular the natural-looking `0 < q ∧ q ≠ 1`\nis **stronger than required** — negative `q` is fine as long as `q ≠ -1` — while a bare\n`q ≠ 0` is **too weak**, since `q = -1` genuinely degenerates the seminormal form (see the\n`S1` discussion in `SeminormalParam.lean`).\n\n**The two halves now live apart.**  This used to be proved here by `Real.log`, which fused\n\"`q ≠ 0 ∧ q² ≠ 1` implies `q` is not a root of unity\" (an `ℝ` fact, and the *only* place the\n`ℝ` in the Wedderburn cone is doing real work) with \"not a root of unity implies no nonzero\npower is `1`\" (true over any field).  They are now\n`GenericParameter.ofSqNeOne` and `GenericParameter.zpow_ne_one` respectively, so the\nstatement below is unchanged and all ~40 call sites are untouched, while the `ℝ`-specific\nstep is isolated and named.  Bean `qou-j377`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.one_add_seminormalParam_mul_neg_of_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.one_add_seminormalParam_mul_neg_of_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L193-L200","doc":"**The determinant factor vanishes at either scalar distance.**  The form the smaller\norbits consume: whenever a generator fixes a tableau, `1 + a(d)a(-d) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_one","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L188-L191","doc":"The positive scalar distance takes the plain branch. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_of_not_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_of_not_neg","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L183-L186","doc":"Off the negative branch, `offDiag` is the plain `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L170-L181","doc":"**The off-diagonal entry vanishes at the scalar distances.**  `offDiag q (-1) = 0` and\n`offDiag q 1 = 1`, so a generator that *fixes* a tableau contributes a genuinely diagonal\nrow — even though the entry rule offers it an off-diagonal slot.\n\nThis is the coherence condition for the smaller orbits: the uniform rule in `genMat` would\ngive a size-`1` orbit an off-diagonal partner entry, and the construction only works because\n`seminormalParam_mul_neg_one` forces that entry to `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_mul_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_mul_neg","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L156-L168","doc":"**The two off-diagonal entries of a size-`2` pair multiply to `1 + a(d)·a(-d)`.**  One\nof them is the plain `1` and the other carries the whole factor — which one depends on the\norientation, and the product does not.  This is the determinant condition in disguise: the\nblock has trace `a(d) + a(-d) = q - q⁻¹` and determinant\n`a(d)a(-d) - (1 + a(d)a(-d)) = -1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_swap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_swap","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L136-L142","doc":"The swap-partner entry, when the swap is admissible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_apply_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_apply_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L127-L134","doc":"Off the diagonal and away from the swap partner, the entry is `0`: each column has at\nmost two nonzero entries. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_of_not_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_of_not_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L119-L125","doc":"Off the diagonal, an inadmissible generator contributes nothing — the size-`1` orbits\nare exactly the scalar rows. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_diag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat_diag","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L112-L117","doc":"The diagonal entry is the seminormal parameter at the tableau's own axial distance. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.genMat","kind":"def","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L96-L110","doc":"**The matrix of `σ_k` on all of `SYT μ`.**  Rows and columns are indexed by standard\ntableaux directly, so no basis correspondence is needed.\n\nThe diagonal is `a(axialDist T k)` for *every* `T`, which is exactly why the trichotomy is a\ncase split on block size and not on the parameter: at distance `+1` this is `q` and at `-1`\nit is `-q⁻¹` (`seminormalParam_one` / `_neg_one`), the two size-`1` orbit scalars, while at\n`|d| ≥ 2` it is the diagonal of a `seminormalBlock`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag","kind":"def","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L86-L94","doc":"The off-diagonal entry of a size-`2` orbit pair, at axial distance `d` **as measured in\nthe source tableau**.  The negative-distance member is the \"first\" of the pair and its\ncolumn carries `1 + a(d)·a(-d)`; the other carries the plain `1`.\n\nThe asymmetry is not a choice of presentation: `seminormalBlock` is the square-root-free\nform, which is not symmetric under exchanging the pair, and mixing the two orientations\nbreaks the braid relation (recorded in `Orbit6Braid`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.instDecEqSYT","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.instDecEqSYT","kind":"instance","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalRep.lean#L75-L84","doc":"`Matrix`'s ring structure needs decidable equality on the index type as well as\nfiniteness — it is what makes the identity matrix definable.\n\nThe **`Fintype` is deliberately not redeclared here**: `YoungTableaux.lean` already provides\n`instFintypeStandardYoungTableau`, which is in scope transitively, and a second\n`Fintype.ofFinite` instance on the same type would be a genuine diamond — `dLambda` is\n*defined* as `Fintype.card (SYT lam)`, so two instances would give two cardinals and break\nany reindexing along `Fintype.equivFin`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}